", "children": [44030430, 44030452], "created_at": "2025-05-19T11:06:59Z", "created_at_i": 1747652819, "num_comments": 2, "objectID": "44028483", "points": 45, "story_id": 44028483, "title": "Monitoring Node.js: Key Metrics You Should Track", "updated_at": "2025-05-20T06:49:34Z", "url": "https://last9.io/blog/node-js-key-metrics/"}, {"_highlightResult": {"author": {"matchLevel": "none", "matchedWords": [], "value": "smitec"}, "title": {"fullyHighlighted": false, "matchLevel": "full", "matchedWords": ["metr"], "value": "If your customers don't talk, NPS is a vanity <em>metr</em>ic"}, "url": {"fullyHighlighted": false, "matchLevel": "full", "matchedWords": ["metr"], "value": "https://www.elliotcsmith.com/if-your-customers-dont-talk-nps-is-a-vanity-<em>metr</em>ic/"}}, "_tags": ["story", "author_smitec", "story_42926845"], "author": "smitec", "children": [42928724, 42969539, 42969619, 42969683, 42969788, 42969949, 42969959, 42970060, 42970184, 42970192, 42970583, 42970731, 42970770, 42970784, 42970868, 42981114], "created_at": "2025-02-04T02:22:55Z", "created_at_i": 1738635775, "num_comments": 55, "objectID": "42926845", "points": 38, "story_id": 42926845, "title": "If your customers don't talk, NPS is a vanity metric", "updated_at": "2025-02-09T22:36:43Z", "url": "https://www.elliotcsmith.com/if-your-customers-dont-talk-nps-is-a-vanity-metric/"}, {"_highlightResult": {"author": {"matchLevel": "none", "matchedWords": [], "value": "dsmurrell"}, "story_text": {"matchLevel": "none", "matchedWords": [], "value": "Hi all!<p>I built Spikelog because I kept wanting to track simple numbers over time but every time I looked at proper observability tools, I'd bounce off the setup complexity. I wanted to make something that didn't require a lot of thinking to use.<p>Spikelog is made to be as simple as possible:<p>- POST a JSON with chart name + value (you can add some tags as well but I've not tested this part works yet)<p>- Chart appears automatically<p>- 1,000 point rolling window per chart (old data expires, no retention config)<p>- Max 10 charts<p>That's basically the whole product.<p>I built it in about a day using Cursor. The API is intentionally minimal so AI assistants can use it too.<p>I have a prompt that lets your coding agent analyze a codebase and add tracking automatically (after you approve the plan).<p>I used it to make Spikelog track itself: <a href=\"https://spikelog.com/p/spikelog\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://spikelog.com/p/spikelog</a><p>There's no alerting yet (that's next), no complex aggregations, no dashboards beyond the auto-generated charts. If you need real observability use something fully featured like Axiom or Datadog. This is for people who just want to see if a number went up or down and don't want to build that themselves. i.e. they want something slightly better than just logging the number.<p>You can also share the charts publicly and I might add some password protection if there is demand for that.<p>I haven't battle-tested it under heavy load. The rolling window deletion is naive (deletes oldest points on insert). There are probably edge cases I haven't hit yet.<p>Would love feedback, especially if you try it and hit something broken."}, "title": {"fullyHighlighted": false, "matchLevel": "full", "matchedWords": ["metr"], "value": "Show HN: Spikelog \u2013 A simple <em>metr</em>ics service for scripts, cron jobs, and MVPs"}, "url": {"matchLevel": "none", "matchedWords": [], "value": "https://spikelog.com"}}, "_tags": ["story", "author_dsmurrell", "story_46068138", "show_hn"], "author": "dsmurrell", "children": [46072991, 46079400, 46079452, 46079466, 46080262, 46085379], "created_at": "2025-11-27T11:10:51Z", "created_at_i": 1764241851, "num_comments": 17, "objectID": "46068138", "points": 36, "story_id": 46068138, "story_text": "Hi all!<p>I built Spikelog because I kept wanting to track simple numbers over time but every time I looked at proper observability tools, I&#x27;d bounce off the setup complexity. I wanted to make something that didn&#x27;t require a lot of thinking to use.<p>Spikelog is made to be as simple as possible:<p>- POST a JSON with chart name + value (you can add some tags as well but I&#x27;ve not tested this part works yet)<p>- Chart appears automatically<p>- 1,000 point rolling window per chart (old data expires, no retention config)<p>- Max 10 charts<p>That&#x27;s basically the whole product.<p>I built it in about a day using Cursor. The API is intentionally minimal so AI assistants can use it too.<p>I have a prompt that lets your coding agent analyze a codebase and add tracking automatically (after you approve the plan).<p>I used it to make Spikelog track itself: <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;spikelog.com&#x2F;p&#x2F;spikelog\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;spikelog.com&#x2F;p&#x2F;spikelog</a><p>There&#x27;s no alerting yet (that&#x27;s next), no complex aggregations, no dashboards beyond the auto-generated charts. If you need real observability use something fully featured like Axiom or Datadog. This is for people who just want to see if a number went up or down and don&#x27;t want to build that themselves. i.e. they want something slightly better than just logging the number.<p>You can also share the charts publicly and I might add some password protection if there is demand for that.<p>I haven&#x27;t battle-tested it under heavy load. The rolling window deletion is naive (deletes oldest points on insert). There are probably edge cases I haven&#x27;t hit yet.<p>Would love feedback, especially if you try it and hit something broken.", "title": "Show HN: Spikelog \u2013 A simple metrics service for scripts, cron jobs, and MVPs", "updated_at": "2026-03-09T18:23:25Z", "url": "https://spikelog.com"}, {"_highlightResult": {"author": {"matchLevel": "none", "matchedWords": [], "value": "mmcclure"}, "title": {"fullyHighlighted": false, "matchLevel": "full", "matchedWords": ["metr"], "value": "San Francisco Solved <em>Metr</em>o Vandalism with One 